  "response": "준비한 내용을 충분히 전달하지 못했다는 느낌이 남아서 그때의 부끄러움이 아직 남아 있는 것 같아요. 그리고 그 감정이 연구가 잘 안 풀리는 상황과 연결되어 있다는 걸 스스로도 알아차리고 계시네요.\n\n발표할 때 정리가 안 된 느낌이 들었던 순간, 구체적으로 어떤 부분이 막혔던 건가요?",
  "reasoning": "The user is a counseling client who shared about a recent presentation where they felt they didn't explain their experimental comparison criteria well enough. They felt embarrassed, worried about what their professor thought, and mentioned that their research hasn't been going well lately, which has made them more sensitive/anxious.\n\nLet me think about what would be helpful here:\n\n1. They're experiencing embarrassment/shame about the presentation\n2. They're ruminating about the professor's reaction (which they can't know)\n3. They're connecting this to broader research difficulties and increased sensitivity\n4. They showed self-awareness by noticing the connection between research stress and their reaction\n\nWhat should I do?\n- Validate their feelings without being dismissive\n- Acknowledge the self-awareness they showed in connecting research stress to their sensitivity\n- Gently note the uncertainty about the professor's reaction without being preachy\n- Maybe explore what \"not going well\" means for them, or what would feel helpful right now\n- Not rush to reassure (\"I'm sure the professor didn't think anything!\") because that dismisses their experience\n- Not push them to \"just talk to the professor\" or take action prematurely\n\nI think a good response would:\n- Acknowledge the discomfort of that moment\n- Reflect back their insight about the connection between research stress and sensitivity\n- Maybe gently open a door to explore what \"not going well\" means, since that seems to be the deeper issue underlying the presentation anxiety\n\nLet me write something natural, warm, and not overly structured.\n",
